When working with large data tables, displaying all available information at once can often make the table overwhelming and hard to read. As a result, it’s common to hide secondary information and use a design pattern to reveal more details only when needed.

Data table design is central to good user experience (UX), especially when handling high-volume or complex data. According to articles like [Why does table design UX matter?](https://www.pencilandpaper.io/articles/ux-pattern-analysis-enterprise-data-tables) and [Design better data tables](https://coyleandrew.medium.com/design-better-data-tables-4ecc99d23356), there’s a consensus on three main ways to manage these hidden details:

- **Expandable Rows** – Expands table rows inline to reveal additional details.
- **Modal Views** – Opens a modal dialog with more in-depth information.
- **Quick View (Sidebar)** – Displays extra details in a sidebar that can slide in from the edge.

In this article, I’ll show you how to set up these three options flexibly, so you can switch between them without needing to rewrite the table’s markup. 

For this, we’ll use **popovers**, which are ideal for easily attaching information directly to specific rows.

---

## Setting Up the Basic Table

To start, we’ll use the same markup and styles as in [A Guide to Styling Tables](https://dev.to/madsstoumann/a-guide-to-styling-tables-28d2):

![Basic Table](assets/2074710/e2a7148a.png)

In the last cell, after the text content, we’ve added a simple button (the icon is from [tabler](https://tabler.io/icons)), that will trigger a popover:

```html
<button type="button" popovertarget="p1"></button>
```

Next, we **add** the popover somewhere in the document to display the additional content when triggered:

```html
<div id="p1" popover>
  ... content ...	
</div>
```

## Styling the Popover

For the popover styling, we’ll use the template from Adam Argyle’s [Steal this popover code](https://nerdy.dev/steal-this-popover-starter-kit). This template includes smooth transitions for the popover’s appearance and disappearance:

```css
.selector {
  &, &::backdrop {
    transition: 
      display .5s allow-discrete, 
      overlay .5s allow-discrete, 
      inset .5s,
      scale .5s,
      opacity .5s;
    opacity: 0;
  }

  &::backdrop {
    background: #0002;
  }

  &:popover-open {
    opacity: 1;
    &::backdrop {
      opacity: 0.5;
    }
  }

  @starting-style {
    &:popover-open,
    &:popover-open::backdrop {
      opacity: 0;
    }
  }
}
```

### Modal View

For the modal layout, we’ll add a `--modal` modifier class to define the modal’s size and center it on the screen:

```css
&.--modal {
  block-size: calc(100dvh - 2em);
  inline-size: calc(100dvw - 2em);
  inset-block-start: 1em;
  inset-inline: 1em;
  transform-origin: 50% 50%;

  @starting-style {
    &:popover-open{
      scale: 0;
    }
  }
}
```

![Modal](assets/2074710/49cf7e6e.png)

---

### Quick View (Sidebar)
To create a quick view sidebar, we'll add a new modifier class, `--inline-start`, to the popover. This sidebar slides in from the side of the viewport and can be closed with a smooth animation. Here’s the setup:

```css
&.--inline-start {
  --width: clamp(220px, 33vw, 350px);
  block-size: calc(100dvh - 2em);
  inline-size: var(--width);
  inset-block-start: 1em;
  inset-inline: 1em;

  @starting-style {
    &:popover-open {
      inset-inline: calc(0px - var(--width));
    }
  }
  &[popover]:not(:popover-open) { 
    inset-inline: calc(0px - var(--ui-width));
  }
}
```

With this setup, the popover slides in from the left. The line `&[popover]:not(:popover-open)` ensures that it slides back out when closed.

![Inline Start](assets/2074710/c4269cd1.png)

We can apply similar adjustments to create slide-in effects from the right or from above and below:

#### Inline End (`--inline-end`)
![Inline End](assets/2074710/43c50e11.png)

#### Block End (`--block-end`)
![Block End](assets/2074710/1f1e781f.png)

